{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NCref6WihCNuSBhD5hJ2ML","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{ProgressProbe: Temporal-Aware Weak Supervision\\\\for Window-Level Progress Estimation in LLM Agent Trajectories}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{Anonymous}\n16\t\\date{}\n17\t\n18\t\\begin{document}\n19\t\\maketitle\n20\t\n21\t%==============================================================================\n22\t\\begin{abstract}\n23\tLLM-based agents frequently exhibit \\emph{semantic stagnation}---executing superficially diverse but collectively ineffective action sequences during long-horizon tasks.\n24\tDetecting such stagnation at the window level requires labels that are expensive to obtain manually and noisy when generated automatically via labeling functions (LFs).\n25\tWe observe that LFs on sequential agent trajectories violate the i.i.d.\\ assumption underlying standard data programming: some LFs \\emph{lead} stagnation onset (e.g., command repetition fires ${\\sim}1.8$ steps early) while others \\emph{lag} (e.g., outcome back-propagation fires ${\\sim}2.1$ steps late).\n26\tExploiting this, we propose a \\textbf{temporal label model} that weights LF votes according to their empirically estimated temporal roles, improving gold-set label quality by $+6$\\,pp macro-F1 over majority vote and $+3$\\,pp over FlyingSquid.\n27\tA lightweight GRU \\textbf{ProgressProbe} trained on these labels achieves $0.80$ macro-F1 on a 400-window human-annotated gold set, matching or slightly exceeding the LF oracle ($+2$\\,pp) without requiring LF computation at inference.\n28\tCompared to LLM-as-judge with chain-of-thought prompting, the probe attains comparable accuracy ($-2$\\,pp vs.\\ best LLM CoT) at $700\\times$ lower latency and ${\\sim}850\\times$ lower cost.\n29\tCross-agent transfer from SWE-agent to OpenHands shows the probe degrades less than the LF oracle ($-9$\\,pp vs.\\ $-14$\\,pp), suggesting it captures implementation-agnostic progress signals.\n30\tExtensive ablations over encoder choice, window size, architecture, and LF contributions confirm the robustness of the approach.\n31\t\\end{abstract}\n32\t\n33\t%==============================================================================\n34\t\\section{Introduction}\n35\t\\label{sec:intro}\n36\t\n37\tLarge language model (LLM) agents are increasingly deployed on complex, long-horizon software engineering tasks, where a single trajectory may span dozens of tool invocations, file edits, and test executions.\n38\tA recurring failure mode is \\emph{semantic stagnation}: the agent produces actions that are individually plausible and superficially diverse, yet collectively fail to advance the task goal.\n39\tUnlike simple repetition loops, semantic stagnation evades cycle detectors because each action differs syntactically from its predecessors.\n40\t\n41\tExisting monitoring approaches fall into several categories, none of which fully address window-level progress estimation.\n42\tCycle detectors~\\citep{chen2024cdcs} capture structural repetition but miss semantically diverse stagnation.\n43\tProcess reward models (PRMs)~\\citep{lightman2023lets,wang2024mathshepherd} score individual steps but require costly per-step outcome labels and lose temporal dynamics within windows.\n44\tSelf-reflection methods~\\citep{shinn2023reflexion,yao2023tree} rely on the stagnating model's own judgment.\n45\tLLM-as-judge approaches provide high accuracy but at latencies ($>2$\\,s/window) and costs (\\$0.50+/1K windows) incompatible with real-time monitoring.\n46\t\n47\tWe reformulate the problem as \\textbf{window-level progress estimation} and propose a three-layer solution.\n48\tFirst, we design five labeling functions capturing complementary stagnation signals (command repetition, embedding diversity drop, error patterns, tool misuse, and outcome back-propagation) and analyze their temporal dynamics relative to stagnation onset (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_dynamics}).\n49\tWe find that LFs exhibit systematic leading/lagging behavior, violating the conditional independence assumption of standard data programming.\n50\tSecond, we propose a \\textbf{temporal label model} (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_lm}) that exploits these temporal roles for improved label aggregation, outperforming majority vote by $+6$\\,pp and FlyingSquid by $+3$\\,pp on a human gold set.\n51\tThird, we train a lightweight \\textbf{ProgressProbe}---a 542K-parameter GRU operating on frozen embeddings---that distills weak labels into a fast inference-time classifier (\\S\\ref{sec:probe}), achieving $0.80$ gold macro-F1 at $4$\\,ms/window.\n52\t\n53\tOur contributions are:\n54\t\\begin{enumerate}\n55\t\\item An empirical analysis of LF temporal dynamics on agent trajectories, revealing leading/lagging structure that violates i.i.d.\\ assumptions (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_dynamics}).\n56\t\\item A temporal-aware label aggregation method that exploits domain-specific leading/lagging indicators for improved weak supervision on sequential data (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_lm}).\n57\t\\item A systematic empirical study including cross-implementation transfer, LLM-as-judge comparison, and extensive ablations (\\S\\ref{sec:experiments}).\n58\t\\end{enumerate}\n59\t\n60\t%==============================================================================\n61\t\\section{Related Work}\n62\t\\label{sec:related}\n63\t\n64\t\\paragraph{Agent Failure Detection.}\n65\tCycle detection approaches such as CDCS/CDSA~\\citep{chen2024cdcs} and SpecRA~\\citep{wang2024specra} identify structural repetition in agent traces.\n66\tTrajAD~\\citep{zhang2024trajad} applies anomaly detection to trajectory embeddings.\n67\tThese methods target surface-level repetition and miss semantically diverse stagnation patterns.\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{Process Reward Models.}\n70\tPRMs~\\citep{lightman2023lets,wang2024mathshepherd} assign step-level correctness scores.\n71\tWhile effective in mathematical reasoning, they require expensive per-step outcome labels and aggregate uniformly within windows, losing temporal dynamics.\n72\tOur approach is a weak-supervision alternative that requires no step-level labels.\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Agent Self-Reflection.}\n75\tReflexion~\\citep{shinn2023reflexion}, LATS~\\citep{zhou2023lats}, and Tree of Thoughts~\\citep{yao2023tree} enable agents to critique and revise their actions.\n76\tThese are intervention mechanisms, not monitoring tools, and rely on the stagnating model's own judgment.\n77\t\n78\t\\paragraph{Data Programming and Weak Supervision.}\n79\tSnorkel~\\citep{ratner2017snorkel} introduced programmatic labeling via noisy LFs aggregated by a generative label model.\n80\tFlyingSquid~\\citep{fu2020fast} extends this with efficient triplet-based dependency estimation.\n81\tBoth assume i.i.d.\\ data points, which is violated in sequential agent trajectories.\n82\tOur temporal label model explicitly addresses this gap.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{LLM-as-Judge.}\n85\tUsing LLMs to evaluate other LLM outputs~\\citep{zheng2023judging} achieves high accuracy but at significant latency and cost, making it impractical for real-time trajectory monitoring.\n86\t\n87\t%==============================================================================\n88\t\\section{Method}\n89\t\\label{sec:method}\n90\t\n91\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n92\t\n93\tGiven an agent trajectory $\\tau = (a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_T)$ of $T$ actions, we partition $\\tau$ into overlapping windows of size $k$: $w_i = (a_i, a_{i+1}, \\ldots, a_{i+k-1})$.\n94\tThe task is binary classification: $y_i \\in \\{\\textsc{Progress}, \\textsc{Stagnation}\\}$ for each window $w_i$.\n95\t\n96\t\\subsection{Labeling Functions}\n97\t\\label{sec:lfs}\n98\t\n99\tWe design five LFs capturing complementary stagnation signals:\n100\t\n101\t\\begin{enumerate}\n102\t\\item \\textbf{Command Repetition} ($\\lambda_\\text{cmd}$): Detects repeated shell commands or API calls within a window via exact and fuzzy matching.\n103\t\\item \\textbf{Diversity Drop} ($\\lambda_\\text{div}$): Measures pairwise cosine similarity of action embeddings; fires when mean similarity exceeds a threshold, indicating low semantic diversity.\n104\t\\item \\textbf{Error Pattern} ($\\lambda_\\text{err}$): Identifies recurring error messages or failure patterns across consecutive actions.\n105\t\\item \\textbf{Tool Misuse} ($\\lambda_\\text{tool}$): Detects repeated invocations of tools with invalid arguments or in inappropriate contexts.\n106\t\\item \\textbf{Outcome Back-Propagation} ($\\lambda_\\text{out}$): Uses trajectory-final exit context (exit codes, error messages) to retrospectively label preceding windows.\n107\t\\end{enumerate}\n108\t\n109\t\\subsection{LF Temporal Dynamics}\n110\t\\label{sec:temporal_dynamics}\n111\t\n112\tWe analyze the temporal offset of each LF relative to stagnation onset across 4,820 onset windows.\n113\tDefine the offset $\\delta_\\lambda$ of LF $\\lambda$ as the median number of steps between $\\lambda$'s first firing and the annotated stagnation onset.\n114\tNegative $\\delta$ indicates a \\emph{leading} indicator; positive $\\delta$ indicates a \\emph{lagging} indicator.\n115\t\n116\tTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_dynamics} shows the results.\n117\tCommand repetition leads stagnation by $1.8$ steps (Cohen's $d = 0.62$, $p < 0.001$), while outcome back-propagation lags by $2.1$ steps ($d = 0.45$).\n118\tTwo LF pairs show statistically significant temporal separation: command\\_repetition $\\rightarrow$ outcome\\_bp ($\\Delta = 3.9$ steps, $p < 0.001$) and diversity\\_drop $\\rightarrow$ outcome\\_bp ($\\Delta = 3.3$ steps, $p < 0.001$).\n119\tThe remaining LFs (error\\_pattern, tool\\_misuse) show negligible temporal differentiation ($|d| < 0.15$).\n120\t\n121\t\\begin{table}[t]\n122\t\\centering\n123\t\\caption{LF temporal dynamics relative to stagnation onset ($n = 4{,}820$ onset windows).}\n124\t\\label{tab:temporal_dynamics}\n125\t\\small\n126\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n127\t\\toprule\n128\tLF & Median Offset & Std & Cohen's $d$ & Role & Significant \\\\\n129\t\\midrule\n130\tcommand\\_repetition & $-1.8$ & 0.9 & 0.62 & Leading & Yes \\\\\n131\tdiversity\\_drop & $-1.2$ & 1.1 & 0.31 & Weak lead & Marginal \\\\\n132\ttool\\_misuse & $-0.2$ & 1.0 & 0.08 & Concurrent & No \\\\\n133\terror\\_pattern & $+0.3$ & 0.8 & 0.12 & Concurrent & No \\\\\n134\toutcome\\_bp & $+2.1$ & 1.3 & 0.45 & Lagging & Yes \\\\\n135\t\\bottomrule\n136\t\\end{tabular}\n137\t\\end{table}\n138\t\n139\t\\begin{table}[t]\n140\t\\centering\n141\t\\caption{Significant LF temporal pairs.}\n142\t\\label{tab:temporal_pairs}\n143\t\\small\n144\t\\begin{tabular}{llcc}\n145\t\\toprule\n146\tLeading LF & Lagging LF & $\\Delta$ Steps & $p$-value \\\\\n147\t\\midrule\n148\tcommand\\_repetition & outcome\\_bp & 3.9 & $<0.001$ \\\\\n149\tdiversity\\_drop & outcome\\_bp & 3.3 & $<0.001$ \\\\\n150\t\\bottomrule\n151\t\\end{tabular}\n152\t\\end{table}\n153\t\n154\t\\subsection{Temporal Label Model}\n155\t\\label{sec:temporal_lm}\n156\t\n157\tStandard majority vote treats all LF votes equally regardless of temporal context.\n158\tWe propose temporal-weighted voting that adjusts LF weights based on their empirically estimated temporal roles.\n159\tSpecifically, for each LF $\\lambda_j$ with estimated offset $\\delta_j$, we compute a position-dependent weight $w_j(t)$ that up-weights leading LFs early in a stagnation episode and lagging LFs later.\n160\tThe aggregated soft label for window $w_i$ is:\n161\t\\[\n162\t\\hat{y}_i = \\sigma\\!\\left(\\sum_{j} w_j(t_i)\\, \\lambda_j(w_i)\\right)\n163\t\\]\n164\twhere $t_i$ is the relative position within the trajectory and $\\sigma$ is the sigmoid function.\n165\t\n166\tTable~\\ref{tab:label_quality} compares label quality on the human gold set ($n = 400$).\n167\tThe temporal label model achieves $0.78$ macro-F1, improving over majority vote by $+6$\\,pp and over FlyingSquid by $+3$\\,pp.\n168\tThe gain concentrates in low-confidence (conf$=$1) windows where LFs disagree: temporal LM achieves $0.73$ accuracy vs.\\ MV's $0.62$ ($+11$\\,pp).\n169\tOn high-confidence (conf$\\geq$2) windows where LFs agree, both methods perform similarly ($0.91$ vs.\\ $0.89$).\n170\t\n171\t\\begin{table}[t]\n172\t\\centering\n173\t\\caption{Label quality comparison on the human gold set (400 windows).}\n174\t\\label{tab:label_quality}\n175\t\\small\n176\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccl}\n177\t\\toprule\n178\tLabel Model & Gold F1 & Gold Acc & Conf=1 Acc & Conf$\\geq$2 Acc & Interpretability \\\\\n179\t\\midrule\n180\tMajority Vote & 0.720 & 0.740 & 0.620 & 0.890 & High (trivial) \\\\\n181\tFlyingSquid & 0.750 & 0.770 & 0.670 & 0.895 & Low (latent) \\\\\n182\tTemporal LM & \\textbf{0.780} & \\textbf{0.800} & \\textbf{0.730} & \\textbf{0.910} & High (explicit) \\\\\n183\t\\bottomrule\n184\t\\end{tabular}\n185\t\\end{table}\n186\t\n187\tAn ablation of temporal weighting contributions (Table~\\ref{tab:temporal_ablation}) confirms that the gain is concentrated in two LF pairs: removing the command\\_rep--outcome\\_bp offset reduces F1 by $2.5$\\,pp, and removing the diversity\\_drop--outcome\\_bp offset reduces it by $1.2$\\,pp.\n188\tAll other offsets contribute only $0.4$\\,pp combined.\n189\t\n190\t\\begin{table}[t]\n191\t\\centering\n192\t\\caption{Temporal label model ablation: contribution of temporal weighting by LF pair.}\n193\t\\label{tab:temporal_ablation}\n194\t\\small\n195\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n196\t\\toprule\n197\tAblation & Gold Macro-F1 & $\\Delta$ vs Full \\\\\n198\t\\midrule\n199\tFull Temporal LM & 0.780 & --- \\\\\n200\tRemove cmd\\_rep--outcome\\_bp offset & 0.755 & $-2.5$\\,pp \\\\\n201\tRemove div\\_drop--outcome\\_bp offset & 0.768 & $-1.2$\\,pp \\\\\n202\tRemove all other offsets & 0.776 & $-0.4$\\,pp \\\\\n203\tUniform weights ($=$ MV) & 0.720 & $-6.0$\\,pp \\\\\n204\t\\bottomrule\n205\t\\end{tabular}\n206\t\\end{table}\n207\t\n208\t\\subsection{ProgressProbe}\n209\t\\label{sec:probe}\n210\t\n211\tProgressProbe is a 2-layer GRU (hidden size 256) operating on frozen Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B~\\citep{qwen3embedding} representations of actions within each window.\n212\tThe final hidden state is passed through a linear classifier with cross-entropy loss.\n213\tThe probe has 542K parameters and runs in $4$\\,ms per window at inference.\n214\t\n215\tTraining uses the temporal label model's soft labels on ${\\sim}15$K windows (conf$\\geq$1).\n216\tWe use a learning rate of $5 \\times 10^{-4}$ with cosine annealing, batch size 64, and train for 20 epochs with early stopping on a validation split.\n217\t\n218\t%==============================================================================\n219\t\\section{Experiments}\n220\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n221\t\n222\t\\subsection{Data and Evaluation Setup}\n223\t\n224\t\\paragraph{Trajectory Data.}\n225\tWe collect 54,809 windows ($k=5$) from SWE-agent trajectories on SWE-bench Lite instances.\n226\tWindows are labeled by the five LFs and aggregated using the temporal label model.\n227\tThe stagnation rate in auto-labeled data is 28.5\\%.\n228\t\n229\t\\paragraph{Human Gold Set.}\n230\t\\label{sec:gold_set}\n231\tWe construct a gold set of 400 windows annotated by 3 annotators with stratified sampling by confidence level and MV label (Table~\\ref{tab:annotation}).\n232\tOverall Fleiss' $\\kappa = 0.68$ (substantial agreement).\n233\tAgreement is high for clear progress ($\\kappa = 0.82$) and clear stagnation ($\\kappa = 0.78$), but lower for ambiguous windows ($\\kappa = 0.45$, 18\\% of total).\n234\tThe gold stagnation rate is 38\\%, higher than the auto-labeled rate due to intentional oversampling of ambiguous cases.\n235\tWe report results on both the full gold set and note that $\\kappa = 0.68$ is at the lower boundary of ``substantial agreement''; we discuss implications in \\S\\ref{sec:limitations}.\n236\t\n237\t\\begin{table}[t]\n238\t\\centering\n239\t\\caption{Inter-annotator agreement on the gold set by category.}\n240\t\\label{tab:annotation}\n241\t\\small\n242\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n243\t\\toprule\n244\tCategory & \\#Windows & Fleiss' $\\kappa$ & Agreement \\% \\\\\n245\t\\midrule\n246\tClear Progress & 198 & 0.82 & 91\\% \\\\\n247\tClear Stagnation & 130 & 0.78 & 87\\% \\\\\n248\tAmbiguous & 72 & 0.45 & 62\\% \\\\\n249\t\\midrule\n250\tOverall & 400 & 0.68 & 82\\% \\\\\n251\t\\bottomrule\n252\t\\end{tabular}\n253\t\\end{table}\n254\t\n255\t\\paragraph{Baselines.}\n256\tWe compare against: (1)~\\textbf{Repeat-3}: a rule that flags windows containing $\\geq$3 repeated commands; (2)~\\textbf{CosineSim}: thresholding mean pairwise cosine similarity; (3)~\\textbf{Structural features}: logistic regression and GBDT on 8 numeric features; (4)~\\textbf{LF Oracle}: applying LFs directly at inference with MV or temporal LM aggregation; (5)~\\textbf{FlyingSquid}: correlation-aware label model; (6)~\\textbf{LLM-as-Judge}: Claude Sonnet 3.5 and GPT-4o in zero-shot, few-shot, and CoT settings.\n257\t\n258\t\\subsection{Main Results}\n259\t\\label{sec:main_results}\n260\t\n261\t\\paragraph{Probe vs.\\ Baselines on Gold Set.}\n262\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} presents the main comparison.\n263\tProgressProbe (Temporal LM) achieves $0.80$ gold macro-F1, outperforming the LF Oracle (MV) by $+8$\\,pp and the LF Oracle (Temporal LM) by $+2$\\,pp.\n264\tThe latter demonstrates a modest student-exceeds-teacher effect: the probe, trained on noisy temporal LM labels, slightly surpasses the label model that generated its training data, without requiring LF computation at inference.\n265\t\n266\t\\begin{table}[t]\n267\t\\centering\n268\t\\caption{Main results on the human gold set. ProgressProbe matches LLM CoT accuracy at $700\\times$ lower latency.}\n269\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n270\t\\small\n271\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n272\t\\toprule\n273\tMethod & Gold F1 & F1-Stag & F1-Prog & Latency & Cost/1K \\\\\n274\t\\midrule\n275\tStructural GBDT & 0.680 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n276\tCosineSim (optimal) & 0.583 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n277\tRepeat-3 & 0.630 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n278\tLF Oracle (MV) & 0.720 & --- & --- & req.\\ LFs & --- \\\\\n279\tLF Oracle (Temporal LM) & 0.780 & --- & --- & req.\\ LFs & --- \\\\\n280\t\\midrule\n281\tGPT-4o Zero-shot & 0.710 & 0.632 & 0.788 & 2.8\\,s & \\$0.38 \\\\\n282\tGPT-4o Few-shot & 0.760 & 0.698 & 0.822 & 3.0\\,s & \\$0.48 \\\\\n283\tGPT-4o CoT & 0.800 & 0.751 & 0.849 & 3.4\\,s & \\$0.62 \\\\\n284\tClaude Zero-shot & 0.730 & 0.658 & 0.802 & 3.2\\,s & \\$0.52 \\\\\n285\tClaude Few-shot & 0.780 & 0.721 & 0.839 & 3.5\\,s & \\$0.68 \\\\\n286\tClaude CoT & \\textbf{0.820} & \\textbf{0.775} & \\textbf{0.865} & 4.1\\,s & \\$0.85 \\\\\n287\t\\midrule\n288\tProbe (MV, conf$\\geq$2) & 0.770 & --- & --- & 4\\,ms & \\$0.001 \\\\\n289\tProbe (Temporal LM) & 0.800 & 0.750 & 0.850 & 4\\,ms & \\$0.001 \\\\\n290\t\\bottomrule\n291\t\\end{tabular}\n292\t\\end{table}\n293\t\n294\t\\paragraph{Cost--Accuracy Tradeoff.}\n295\tThe probe achieves comparable accuracy to the best LLM CoT variant (Claude CoT: $0.82$; probe: $0.80$; $\\Delta = -2$\\,pp) while being ${\\sim}700\\times$ faster (4\\,ms vs.\\ 3.2\\,s) and ${\\sim}850\\times$ cheaper (\\$0.001 vs.\\ \\$0.85 per 1K windows).\n296\tFor real-time monitoring of agent trajectories, this cost--accuracy tradeoff strongly favors the probe.\n297\t\n298\t\\paragraph{CosineSim Baseline.}\n299\tThe CosineSim baseline achieves only $0.31$ macro-F1 at the default (median) threshold and $0.58$ even with oracle threshold optimization (Table~\\ref{tab:cosinesim}).\n300\tThe weak correlation between cosine similarity and progress labels ($r = -0.18$) confirms that a single similarity statistic cannot distinguish progress from stagnation: high similarity can indicate either effective sequential actions or repetitive ineffective ones.\n301\t\n302\t\\begin{table}[t]\n303\t\\centering\n304\t\\caption{CosineSim threshold sensitivity analysis.}\n305\t\\label{tab:cosinesim}\n306\t\\small\n307\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n308\t\\toprule\n309\tThreshold & Precision & Recall & Macro-F1 \\\\\n310\t\\midrule\n311\t0.91 (median) & 0.42 & 0.38 & 0.313 \\\\\n312\t0.90 & 0.48 & 0.51 & 0.470 \\\\\n313\t0.872 (optimal) & 0.57 & 0.60 & 0.583 \\\\\n314\t0.85 & 0.53 & 0.64 & 0.550 \\\\\n315\t\\bottomrule\n316\t\\end{tabular}\n317\t\\end{table}\n318\t\n319\t\\paragraph{Non-NLP Structural Baseline.}\n320\tStructural features alone (lines added/deleted, file count, command count, etc.) achieve at most $0.71$ macro-F1 with GBDT (Table~\\ref{tab:structural}), $19$\\,pp below the probe.\n321\tThis confirms that progress estimation requires semantic understanding of action content and justifies the use of NL embeddings.\n322\t\n323\t\\begin{table}[t]\n324\t\\centering\n325\t\\caption{Non-NLP structural baselines.}\n326\t\\label{tab:structural}\n327\t\\small\n328\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n329\t\\toprule\n330\tMethod & Auto Macro-F1 & Gold Macro-F1 & Features \\\\\n331\t\\midrule\n332\tStructural LR & 0.682 & 0.650 & 8 numeric \\\\\n333\tStructural GBDT & 0.714 & 0.680 & 8 numeric \\\\\n334\tRepeat-3 & 0.843 & 0.630 & 1 rule \\\\\n335\tProbe (NL embedding) & \\textbf{0.906} & \\textbf{0.800} & 1024-dim \\\\\n336\t\\bottomrule\n337\t\\end{tabular}\n338\t\\end{table}\n339\t\n340\t\\subsection{Multi-Seed Stability}\n341\t\\label{sec:stability}\n342\t\n343\tTable~\\ref{tab:multiseed} reports probe performance across 3 random seeds on the conf$\\geq$2 auto-labeled test set.\n344\tMean macro-F1 is $0.919 \\pm 0.011$, with all seeds maintaining $>5$\\,pp gap over Repeat-3 (range $0.62$--$0.84$).\n345\tThe low variance confirms training stability.\n346\t\n347\t\\begin{table}[t]\n348\t\\centering\n349\t\\caption{Multi-seed probe results on conf$\\geq$2 auto-labeled test set.}\n350\t\\label{tab:multiseed}\n351\t\\small\n352\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n353\t\\toprule\n354\tSeed & Macro-F1 & Accuracy & F1-Stag & F1-Prog \\\\\n355\t\\midrule\n356\t42 & 0.906 & 0.928 & 0.859 & 0.952 \\\\\n357\t123 & 0.927 & 0.940 & 0.881 & 0.973 \\\\\n358\t456 & 0.923 & 0.937 & 0.876 & 0.970 \\\\\n359\t\\midrule\n360\tMean$\\pm$Std & $0.919{\\pm}0.011$ & $0.935{\\pm}0.006$ & $0.872{\\pm}0.012$ & $0.965{\\pm}0.011$ \\\\\n361\t\\bottomrule\n362\t\\end{tabular}\n363\t\\end{table}\n364\t\n365\t\\subsection{Cross-Agent Transfer}\n366\t\\label{sec:transfer}\n367\t\n368\tWe evaluate zero-shot transfer of the SWE-agent--trained probe to 1,247 OpenHands trajectories (18,340 windows).\n369\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_agent} shows results.\n370\tThe probe degrades by $9$\\,pp (from $0.80$ to $0.71$ gold F1), while the LF Oracle (Temporal LM) degrades by $14$\\,pp (from $0.78$ to $0.64$).\n371\tThe probe's smaller degradation suggests it captures more implementation-agnostic progress signals through embeddings, whereas rule-based LFs are tied to SWE-agent--specific patterns.\n372\t\n373\t\\begin{table}[t]\n374\t\\centering\n375\t\\caption{Cross-agent transfer: SWE-agent $\\rightarrow$ OpenHands.}\n376\t\\label{tab:cross_agent}\n377\t\\small\n378\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n379\t\\toprule\n380\tMethod & In-domain (Gold) & Cross-agent & Degradation \\\\\n381\t\\midrule\n382\tLF Oracle (MV) & 0.720 & 0.640 & $-8.0$\\,pp \\\\\n383\tLF Oracle (Temporal LM) & 0.780 & 0.640 & $-14.0$\\,pp \\\\\n384\tProbe (Temporal LM) & 0.800 & 0.710 & $-9.0$\\,pp \\\\\n385\tRepeat-3 & 0.630 & 0.580 & $-5.0$\\,pp \\\\\n386\t\\bottomrule\n387\t\\end{tabular}\n388\t\\end{table}\n389\t\n390\tPer-LF transfer analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:lf_transfer}) reveals that embedding-based LFs (diversity\\_drop) transfer well (precision $0.81 \\rightarrow 0.74$), while implementation-specific LFs (outcome\\_bp, tool\\_misuse) transfer poorly (precision drops to $0.41$ and $0.39$ respectively), explaining the LF oracle's larger degradation.\n391\t\n392\t\\begin{table}[t]\n393\t\\centering\n394\t\\caption{Per-LF transfer quality from SWE-agent to OpenHands.}\n395\t\\label{tab:lf_transfer}\n396\t\\small\n397\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n398\t\\toprule\n399\tLF & In-domain Prec & Cross-agent Prec & Transfer Quality \\\\\n400\t\\midrule\n401\tcommand\\_repetition & 0.85 & 0.62 & Partial \\\\\n402\toutcome\\_bp & 0.78 & 0.41 & Poor \\\\\n403\tdiversity\\_drop & 0.81 & 0.74 & Good \\\\\n404\terror\\_pattern & 0.76 & 0.58 & Partial \\\\\n405\ttool\\_misuse & 0.72 & 0.39 & Poor \\\\\n406\t\\bottomrule\n407\t\\end{tabular}\n408\t\\end{table}\n409\t\n410\t\\subsection{FlyingSquid Comparison}\n411\t\\label{sec:flyingsquid}\n412\t\n413\tFlyingSquid~\\citep{fu2020fast} achieves $0.75$ gold macro-F1 ($+3$\\,pp over MV), identifying 4 LF dependency edges via the triplet method (Table~\\ref{tab:label_quality}).\n414\tThe temporal label model exceeds FlyingSquid by $+3$\\,pp.\n415\tWhile this margin is moderate, the temporal LM offers two qualitative advantages: (1)~interpretable temporal roles for each LF (leading/lagging) vs.\\ FlyingSquid's latent dependency structure, and (2)~explicit modeling of sequential structure rather than treating windows as i.i.d.\n416\tA probe trained on FlyingSquid labels achieves $0.78$ gold F1, $2$\\,pp below our temporal LM--trained probe.\n417\t\n418\t\\subsection{Outcome Back-Propagation Sensitivity}\n419\t\\label{sec:outcome_bp}\n420\t\n421\tThe outcome\\_bp LF uses trajectory-final information to retrospectively label earlier windows, raising a potential information leakage concern.\n422\tWe quantify this via holdout experiments (Table~\\ref{tab:outcome_sensitivity}).\n423\tCompletely removing outcome\\_bp reduces probe gold F1 by $4$\\,pp (from $0.80$ to $0.76$).\n424\tRestricting outcome\\_bp to fire only in the last 3 windows (where 71\\% of its firings naturally occur) reduces the drop to $2$\\,pp while substantially mitigating leakage.\n425\tWithout outcome\\_bp, the temporal label model's advantage over MV shrinks from $+6$\\,pp to $+2$\\,pp, indicating that roughly half of the temporal LM's gain depends on this LF's temporal role.\n426\t\n427\t\\begin{table}[t]\n428\t\\centering\n429\t\\caption{Outcome back-propagation sensitivity analysis.}\n430\t\\label{tab:outcome_sensitivity}\n431\t\\small\n432\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n433\t\\toprule\n434\tConfiguration & Auto F1 & Gold F1 & $\\Delta$ vs Full \\\\\n435\t\\midrule\n436\tFull (all LFs) & 0.906 & 0.800 & --- \\\\\n437\tHold-out outcome\\_bp & 0.862 & 0.760 & $-4.0$\\,pp \\\\\n438\toutcome\\_bp last-3 only & 0.886 & 0.780 & $-2.0$\\,pp \\\\\n439\tTemporal LM w/o outcome\\_bp & --- & 0.740$^*$ & $-4.0$\\,pp$^*$ \\\\\n440\t\\bottomrule\n441\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small $^*$Label quality metric, not probe prediction.}\n442\t\\end{tabular}\n443\t\\end{table}\n444\t\n445\tFor practical deployment, we recommend the last-3-only variant as the default configuration, which preserves most of the signal ($-2$\\,pp) while confining outcome information to the trajectory's final segment.\n446\tIn offline analysis settings where outcome information is available by definition, the full configuration is appropriate.\n447\t\n448\t\\subsection{LF Hold-One-Out Ablation}\n449\t\\label{sec:lf_ablation}\n450\t\n451\tTable~\\ref{tab:lf_holdout} shows the marginal contribution of each LF via hold-one-out retraining.\n452\tOutcome\\_bp ($-3.5$\\,pp) and command\\_repetition ($-2.8$\\,pp) together account for ${\\sim}68\\%$ of the total LF signal.\n453\tThe sum of individual drops ($9.2$\\,pp) exceeds the total gap over MV, indicating substantial inter-LF redundancy---a typical property of data programming systems.\n454\t\n455\t\\begin{table}[t]\n456\t\\centering\n457\t\\caption{LF hold-one-out ablation on auto-labeled test set.}\n458\t\\label{tab:lf_holdout}\n459\t\\small\n460\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n461\t\\toprule\n462\tHeld-out LF & Macro-F1 & Drop (pp) & \\% of Signal \\\\\n463\t\\midrule\n464\tNone (Full) & 0.906 & --- & --- \\\\\n465\toutcome\\_bp & 0.871 & $-3.5$ & 38\\% \\\\\n466\tcommand\\_repetition & 0.878 & $-2.8$ & 30\\% \\\\\n467\tdiversity\\_drop & 0.894 & $-1.2$ & 13\\% \\\\\n468\terror\\_pattern & 0.896 & $-1.0$ & 11\\% \\\\\n469\ttool\\_misuse & 0.899 & $-0.7$ & 8\\% \\\\\n470\t\\bottomrule\n471\t\\end{tabular}\n472\t\\end{table}\n473\t\n474\t\\subsection{Ablation Studies}\n475\t\\label{sec:ablations}\n476\t\n477\t\\paragraph{Window Size.}\n478\tTable~\\ref{tab:window_size} reports probe performance for window sizes $k \\in \\{3, 5, 7\\}$.\n479\t$k = 5$ is optimal (gold F1 $= 0.80$); $k = 3$ degrades by $3.4$\\,pp (insufficient context for temporal patterns to unfold), while $k = 7$ degrades by $1.3$\\,pp (signal dilution within longer windows).\n480\tThe moderate sensitivity to $k$ supports the method's practical robustness.\n481\t\n482\t\\begin{table}[t]\n483\t\\centering\n484\t\\caption{Window size ablation.}\n485\t\\label{tab:window_size}\n486\t\\small\n487\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n488\t\\toprule\n489\t$k$ & Auto Macro-F1 & Gold Macro-F1 & \\#Windows & $\\Delta$ vs $k{=}5$ \\\\\n490\t\\midrule\n491\t3 & 0.872 & 0.760 & 91,348 & $-3.4$\\,pp \\\\\n492\t5 & \\textbf{0.906} & \\textbf{0.800} & 54,809 & --- \\\\\n493\t7 & 0.893 & 0.790 & 39,149 & $-1.3$\\,pp \\\\\n494\t\\bottomrule\n495\t\\end{tabular}\n496\t\\end{table}\n497\t\n498\t\\paragraph{Architecture.}\n499\tTable~\\ref{tab:architecture} compares four architectures.\n500\tThe 2-layer GRU achieves the best tradeoff between capacity and data efficiency.\n501\tAdding attention yields only $+0.6$\\,pp at the cost of additional complexity.\n502\tTransformer overfits ($-2.3$\\,pp) due to its larger parameter count (1.2M vs.\\ 542K) on ${\\sim}15$K training windows.\n503\tMLP on mean-pooled features drops $4.9$\\,pp, directly demonstrating the value of sequential modeling.\n504\t\n505\t\\begin{table}[t]\n506\t\\centering\n507\t\\caption{Architecture ablation.}\n508\t\\label{tab:architecture}\n509\t\\small\n510\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n511\t\\toprule\n512\tArchitecture & Auto F1 & Gold F1 & \\#Params & $\\Delta$ vs GRU \\\\\n513\t\\midrule\n514\tMLP (mean-pool) & 0.857 & 0.760 & 263K & $-4.9$\\,pp \\\\\n515\tTransformer & 0.883 & 0.780 & 1,247K & $-2.3$\\,pp \\\\\n516\tGRU (default) & \\textbf{0.906} & \\textbf{0.800} & 542K & --- \\\\\n517\tGRU + Attention & 0.912 & 0.810 & 609K & $+0.6$\\,pp \\\\\n518\t\\bottomrule\n519\t\\end{tabular}\n520\t\\end{table}\n521\t\n522\t\\paragraph{Encoder Choice.}\n523\tTable~\\ref{tab:encoder} compares four embedding models, all with 1024-dimensional output.\n524\tPerformance ranges from $0.78$ to $0.80$ gold F1 (max spread $2$\\,pp), indicating the method is not critically dependent on encoder choice.\n525\tQwen3-Embedding-0.6B achieves the best performance, likely due to stronger code/technical content in its training data.\n526\t\n527\t\\begin{table}[t]\n528\t\\centering\n529\t\\caption{Encoder ablation.}\n530\t\\label{tab:encoder}\n531\t\\small\n532\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n533\t\\toprule\n534\tEncoder & Auto F1 & Gold F1 & Embed Time & $\\Delta$ vs Qwen3 \\\\\n535\t\\midrule\n536\tQwen3-Embedding-0.6B & \\textbf{0.906} & \\textbf{0.800} & 53\\,min & --- \\\\\n537\tGTE-large & 0.894 & 0.790 & 45\\,min & $-1.2$\\,pp \\\\\n538\tBGE-M3 & 0.891 & 0.790 & 48\\,min & $-1.5$\\,pp \\\\\n539\tE5-large-v2 & 0.886 & 0.780 & 42\\,min & $-2.0$\\,pp \\\\\n540\t\\bottomrule\n541\t\\end{tabular}\n542\t\\end{table}\n543\t\n544\t\\paragraph{Data Scaling.}\n545\tFigure~\\ref{tab:scaling} shows diminishing returns beyond 25\\% of training data (${\\sim}3{,}850$ windows), which achieves 95\\% of full performance.\n546\tEven with 10\\% of data ($1{,}540$ windows), the probe reaches $0.72$ gold F1, matching the MV baseline.\n547\t\n548\t\\begin{table}[t]\n549\t\\centering\n550\t\\caption{Data scaling curve.}\n551\t\\label{tab:scaling}\n552\t\\small\n553\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n554\t\\toprule\n555\tTrain \\% & \\#Windows & Auto F1 & Gold F1 & $\\Delta$ vs 100\\% \\\\\n556\t\\midrule\n557\t10\\% & 1,540 & 0.811 & 0.720 & $-9.5$\\,pp \\\\\n558\t25\\% & 3,850 & 0.858 & 0.760 & $-4.8$\\,pp \\\\\n559\t50\\% & 7,700 & 0.887 & 0.780 & $-1.9$\\,pp \\\\\n560\t100\\% & 15,401 & 0.906 & 0.800 & --- \\\\\n561\t\\bottomrule\n562\t\\end{tabular}\n563\t\\end{table}\n564\t\n565\t\\paragraph{SupCon Auxiliary Loss.}\n566\tAdding supervised contrastive loss ($\\alpha = 0.5$) improves auto F1 by $0.5$\\,pp and gold F1 by $1$\\,pp, with improved embedding silhouette score ($0.42 \\rightarrow 0.52$) at $35\\%$ additional training time (Table~\\ref{tab:supcon}).\n567\tThe marginal gain does not justify the added complexity for the default configuration.\n568\t\n569\t\\begin{table}[t]\n570\t\\centering\n571\t\\caption{Supervised contrastive loss ablation.}\n572\t\\label{tab:supcon}\n573\t\\small\n574\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n575\t\\toprule\n576\tConfiguration & Auto F1 & Gold F1 & Silhouette & Train Time \\\\\n577\t\\midrule\n578\tCE only (default) & 0.906 & 0.800 & 0.42 & $1.0\\times$ \\\\\n579\tCE + SupCon $\\alpha{=}0.5$ & 0.911 & 0.810 & 0.52 & $1.35\\times$ \\\\\n580\tCE + SupCon $\\alpha{=}1.0$ & 0.907 & 0.805 & 0.55 & $1.35\\times$ \\\\\n581\t\\bottomrule\n582\t\\end{tabular}\n583\t\\end{table}\n584\t\n585\t\\subsection{Error Analysis}\n586\t\\label{sec:error_analysis}\n587\t\n588\tOn the gold set ($n = 120$ test windows), the probe produces 16 false positives and 12 false negatives (Table~\\ref{tab:errors}).\n589\tAmong FPs, 42\\% involve exploratory actions misclassified as stagnation---the agent tries new approaches that resemble stagnation in embedding space.\n590\tAmong FNs, 55\\% involve semantically diverse stagnation where the agent executes different but equally ineffective actions, fooling the embedding-based probe.\n591\tNotably, 63\\% of probe errors overlap with human annotator disagreement regions, suggesting these are inherently ambiguous cases rather than systematic model failures.\n592\tCore-region accuracy (non-boundary windows) is $0.94$, while boundary-region accuracy is only $0.58$.\n593\t\n594\t\\begin{table}[t]\n595\t\\centering\n596\t\\caption{Probe error taxonomy on the gold test set.}\n597\t\\label{tab:errors}\n598\t\\small\n599\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n600\t\\toprule\n601\tError Type & Count & \\% of Errors & Dominant Pattern \\\\\n602\t\\midrule\n603\tFalse Positive & 16 & 57\\% & Exploration misclassified (42\\%) \\\\\n604\tFalse Negative & 12 & 43\\% & Semantic diverse stagnation (55\\%) \\\\\n605\t\\bottomrule\n606\t\\end{tabular}\n607\t\\end{table}\n608\t\n609\t\\subsection{Early Termination Simulation}\n610\t\\label{sec:early_termination}\n611\t\n612\tAs a downstream application, we simulate stagnation-triggered early termination on 312 held-out trajectories (avg.\\ 48,500 tokens each).\n613\tAt the optimal threshold ($0.55$), the probe saves 24\\% of tokens with only 3\\% success rate drop and 5\\% false termination rate (Table~\\ref{tab:termination}).\n614\tMore aggressive thresholds ($0.3$) save 42\\% but incur 12\\% success rate drop.\n615\tThis demonstrates the probe's calibration: stagnation confidence monotonically predicts actual stagnation probability.\n616\t\n617\t\\begin{table}[t]\n618\t\\centering\n619\t\\caption{Early termination tradeoff on 312 held-out trajectories.}\n620\t\\label{tab:termination}\n621\t\\small\n622\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n623\t\\toprule\n624\tThreshold & Tokens Saved & Success Drop & False Term.\\ Rate \\\\\n625\t\\midrule\n626\t0.3 & 42\\% & 12\\% & 0.18 \\\\\n627\t0.5 & 28\\% & 5\\% & 0.08 \\\\\n628\t0.55 (optimal) & 24\\% & 3\\% & 0.05 \\\\\n629\t0.7 & 15\\% & 2\\% & 0.03 \\\\\n630\t\\bottomrule\n631\t\\end{tabular}\n632\t\\end{table}\n633\t\n634\t%==============================================================================\n635\t\\section{Discussion}\n636\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n637\t\n638\t\\paragraph{Temporal Label Model as Interpretable Aggregation.}\n639\tThe temporal label model's primary contribution is an interpretable, temporal-aware aggregation mechanism for sequential weak supervision.\n640\tThe $+6$\\,pp gain over MV is meaningful but concentrated in two LF pairs, limiting claims of general-purpose temporal label modeling.\n641\tThe advantage over FlyingSquid ($+3$\\,pp) is moderate; the temporal LM's main differentiator is explicit interpretability of LF temporal roles rather than raw accuracy.\n642\t\n643\t\\paragraph{Practical Deployment.}\n644\tThe probe's $4$\\,ms inference latency enables real-time monitoring of agent trajectories.\n645\tCombined with early termination (24\\% token savings at 3\\% success cost), the system provides a practical tool for agent orchestration.\n646\tFor deployment, we recommend the last-3-only outcome\\_bp variant to mitigate information leakage with minimal performance impact ($-2$\\,pp).\n647\t\n648\t\\paragraph{Representation Geometry.}\n649\tUMAP visualization of GRU hidden states shows class-specific clustering (silhouette $= 0.51$) with a 22\\% boundary region where accuracy drops to $0.58$.\n650\tThis boundary region corresponds closely to annotator disagreement, suggesting that the probe's errors reflect inherent task ambiguity rather than model limitations.\n651\t\n652\t%==============================================================================\n653\t\\section{Limitations}\n654\t\\label{sec:limitations}\n655\t\n656\t\\paragraph{Gold Set Quality.}\n657\t$\\kappa = 0.68$ is at the borderline of substantial agreement.\n658\tAmbiguous windows (18\\%, $\\kappa = 0.45$) lower the overall score and may inflate error rates on the gold set.\n659\t\n660\t\\paragraph{Outcome BP Dependency.}\n661\tThe temporal label model's gain halves (from $+6$\\,pp to $+2$\\,pp vs.\\ MV) when outcome\\_bp is removed, indicating significant dependence on a LF with potential leakage.\n662\t\n663\t\\paragraph{Cross-Agent Degradation.}\n664\tWhile the probe transfers better than LF oracles ($-9$\\,pp vs.\\ $-14$\\,pp), absolute cross-agent F1 of $0.71$ is below acceptable deployment thresholds without further adaptation.\n665\t\n666\t\\paragraph{Scope.}\n667\tBoth in-domain and cross-agent evaluations involve SE agents (SWE-agent, OpenHands).\n668\tGeneralization to non-SE domains (web browsing, data analysis) is untested.\n669\t\n670\t\\paragraph{Temporal LM Generality.}\n671\tOnly 2 of 10 possible LF pairs show significant temporal separation, limiting the claim that temporal awareness is broadly beneficial for data programming on sequential data.\n672\t\n673\t%==============================================================================\n674\t\\section{Conclusion}\n675\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n676\t\n677\tWe presented ProgressProbe, a system for window-level progress estimation in LLM agent trajectories.\n678\tOur temporal label model exploits the leading/lagging dynamics of labeling functions on sequential data, improving label quality by $+6$\\,pp over majority vote.\n679\tA lightweight GRU probe trained on these labels achieves $0.80$ gold macro-F1 at $4$\\,ms/window---comparable to LLM-as-judge CoT ($-2$\\,pp) at $700\\times$ lower latency and ${\\sim}850\\times$ lower cost.\n680\tCross-agent transfer shows the probe captures more implementation-agnostic signals than rule-based LFs, with smaller degradation ($-9$\\,pp vs.\\ $-14$\\,pp).\n681\tWhile the temporal label model's gains concentrate in a few LF pairs and overall novelty is bounded, the combination of temporal-aware weak supervision, efficient inference, and transferable progress signals constitutes a practical and principled approach to monitoring agent behavior at scale.\n682\t\n683\t%==============================================================================\n684\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n685\t\n686\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n687\t\n688\t\\bibitem[Chen et~al.(2024)]{chen2024cdcs}\n689\tChen, X., et~al. (2024).\n690\t\\newblock CDCS and CDSA: Cycle detection for stagnation in LLM agent trajectories.\n691\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICSE}, pp.\\ 1--12.\n692\t\n693\t\\bibitem[Fu et~al.(2020)]{fu2020fast}\n694\tFu, D.~Y., et~al. (2020).\n695\t\\newblock Fast and three-rious: Speeding up weak supervision with triplet methods.\n696\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICML}, pp.\\ 3280--3291.\n697\t\n698\t\\bibitem[Lightman et~al.(2023)]{lightman2023lets}\n699\tLightman, H., et~al. (2023).\n700\t\\newblock Let's verify step by step.\n701\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2305.20050}.\n702\t\n703\t\\bibitem[Ratner et~al.(2017)]{ratner2017snorkel}\n704\tRatner, A., et~al. (2017).\n705\t\\newblock Snorkel: Rapid training data creation with weak supervision.\n706\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ VLDB}, 11(3):269--282.\n707\t\n708\t\\bibitem[Shinn et~al.(2023)]{shinn2023reflexion}\n709\tShinn, N., et~al. (2023).\n710\t\\newblock Reflexion: Language agents with verbal reinforcement learning.\n711\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}.\n712\t\n713\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2024a)]{wang2024specra}\n714\tWang, Y., et~al. (2024a).\n715\t\\newblock SpecRA: Spectral repetition analysis for agent trajectory monitoring.\n716\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n717\t\n718\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2024b)]{wang2024mathshepherd}\n719\tWang, P., et~al. (2024b).\n720\t\\newblock Math-Shepherd: Verify and reinforce LLMs step-by-step without human annotations.\n721\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ACL}, pp.\\ 9426--9439.\n722\t\n723\t\\bibitem[Yao et~al.(2023)]{yao2023tree}\n724\tYao, S., et~al. (2023).\n725\t\\newblock Tree of thoughts: Deliberate problem solving with large language models.\n726\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}.\n727\t\n728\t\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2024)]{zhang2024trajad}\n729\tZhang, L., et~al. (2024).\n730\t\\newblock TrajAD: Anomaly detection in agent trajectories via embedding analysis.\n731\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n732\t\n733\t\\bibitem[Zheng et~al.(2023)]{zheng2023judging}\n734\tZheng, L., et~al. (2023).\n735\t\\newblock Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena.\n736\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}.\n737\t\n738\t\\bibitem[Zhou et~al.(2023)]{zhou2023lats}\n739\tZhou, A., et~al. (2023).\n740\t\\newblock Language agent tree search unifies reasoning acting and planning in language models.\n741\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.04406}.\n742\t\n743\t\\bibitem[Qwen(2025)]{qwen3embedding}\n744\tQwen Team. (2025).\n745\t\\newblock Qwen3-Embedding: A multilingual embedding model.\n746\t\\newblock Technical report, Alibaba Group.\n747\t\n748\t\\end{thebibliography}\n749\t\n750\t\\end{document}\n751\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Je8BnXYm3AvvVV9jmydn5n","session_id":"50f692d3-19d9-47a7-bdff-a390f713b177","uuid":"151b4f88-a837-4e1e-a84a-efef8f1fea17","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:55:17.953Z"}
