{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VFiWPkMuAQWCDXMCf6esBL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T095527\n3\ttier: median\n4\toverall_score: weak reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T10:52:49.954004+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: ProgressProbe\n9\t\n10\t**Paper**: ProgressProbe: Temporal-Aware Weak Supervision for Window-Level Progress Estimation in LLM Agent Trajectories\n11\t\n12\t**Review Mode**: Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n13\t- R1 Focus: Methodology + Experiments\n14\t- R2 Focus: Novelty + Related Work\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t## 1. Paper Summary\n19\t\n20\tThe paper addresses \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories — where agents produce superficially diverse but collectively ineffective actions — and proposes a three-layer system: (1) five labeling functions (LFs) capturing complementary stagnation signals, (2) a temporal label model that weights LF votes by empirically estimated leading/lagging roles, and (3) ProgressProbe, a lightweight 542K-parameter GRU trained on the resulting weak labels. The probe achieves 0.80 gold macro-F1 on a 400-window human-annotated set at 4ms/window, comparable to LLM-as-judge CoT (0.82) at 700× lower latency and ~850× lower cost.\n21\t\n22\t---\n23\t\n24\t## 2. Consolidated Strengths\n25\t\n26\t**S1. Well-motivated problem with practical significance.** (R1, R2 agree)\n27\tSemantic stagnation — action diversity without progress — is a genuine, underaddressed failure mode in long-horizon agent tasks. The distinction from structural repetition (caught by cycle detectors) is clearly drawn. The window-level framing is operationally actionable.\n28\t\n29\t**S2. Exceptional ablation coverage.** (R1 primary, R2 concurs)\n30\tThe paper presents 20 tables spanning architecture, encoder, window size, data scaling, LF hold-one-out, contrastive loss, cross-agent transfer, outcome_bp sensitivity, multi-seed stability, and error analysis. This level of experimental thoroughness is above average for the venue and provides actionable practitioner insight.\n31\t\n32\t**S3. Cost-efficiency result is the strongest practical contribution.** (R2 primary, R1 concurs)\n33\tThe 700× latency reduction and 850× cost reduction versus LLM CoT at only −2pp accuracy is genuinely compelling for deployment scenarios and gives the work applied value independent of temporal LM novelty.\n34\t\n35\t**S4. Unusually honest limitations section.** (R1, R2 agree)\n36\tThe paper transparently acknowledges: borderline κ=0.68, outcome_bp dependency, cross-agent degradation, SE-only scope, and temporal LM generality limited to 2/10 LF pairs. This self-awareness improves credibility.\n37\t\n38\t**S5. Student-exceeds-teacher framing is appropriately restrained.** (R1)\n39\tThe +2pp probe-over-oracle result is not overclaimed; the paper correctly notes the weak effect size and provides a plausible mechanism.\n40\t\n41\t---\n42\t\n43\t## 3. Consolidated Weaknesses\n44\t\n45\t### Major\n46\t\n47\t**W1. Temporal label model novelty is overstated and empirically fragile.** (R1 W1 + R2 W1 — unanimous)\n48\t\n49\tThe paper's primary methodological claim is a temporal-aware label aggregation method. However:\n50\t- **Empirical fragility**: Table 4 reveals the gain is almost entirely concentrated in 2 of 10 possible LF pairs (cmd_rep–outcome_bp: −2.5pp when ablated; div_drop–outcome_bp: −1.2pp). All other pairs contribute only −0.4pp combined. Table 2 confirms only 2/10 pairs show statistically significant temporal separation. As R1 notes, this may reduce to \"reweight outcome_bp higher\" rather than a general temporal modeling contribution.\n51\t- **Insufficient novelty differentiation**: R2 identifies critical missing citations — position-dependent weighted voting is a known design pattern. Safranchik et al. (2020, \"Weakly Supervised Sequence Tagging from Noisy Rules,\" AAAI), Lison et al. (2021, \"skweak,\" ACL), and Snorkel MeTaL (Ratner et al., 2019) all handle temporal/sequential structure in labeling functions. The paper does not cite or differentiate from these directly competing methods. R1 concurs this gap is substantive.\n52\t\n53\t*Roundtable consensus*: The framework is presented at a generality the evidence does not support. The contribution should be reframed as a domain-specific temporal heuristic for a narrow set of LF interactions, or the empirical scope must be expanded.\n54\t\n55\t**W2. Outcome backpropagation creates a near-circular dependency that is insufficiently addressed.** (R1 W2 — R2 adopts as co-major after cross-sharing)\n56\t\n57\toutcome_bp uses trajectory-final exit context to retrospectively label earlier windows. It accounts for ~44–50% of the gap over MV baseline (Table 13: −3.5pp hold-one-out; Table 12: −4pp full holdout). Critical concerns:\n58\t- At inference time on live agents, outcome_bp is unavailable, yet the paper's deployment claim is real-time monitoring.\n59\t- The \"last-3-only\" variant (Table 12, −2pp) still contributed to temporal model calibration during training — this is not a clean zero-outcome_bp simulation.\n60\t- The temporal LM's gain vs MV drops from +6pp to +2pp without outcome_bp (Table 12), meaning roughly half the temporal LM's value derives from a LF with potential information leakage.\n61\t- R2 raises the additional concern: the +2pp probe-over-oracle effect may partly reflect the probe implicitly learning to replicate outcome_bp's signal from context rather than genuine generalization. A train-without-outcome_bp ablation is conspicuously absent.\n62\t\n63\t**W3. Related work is severely incomplete.** (R2 W2 — R1 concurs after cross-sharing)\n64\t\n65\tOnly 11 papers are cited — anomalously sparse for a top venue submission. Critical omissions identified by R2:\n66\t- Sequential weak supervision: Safranchik et al. (2020), Lison et al. (2021), the broader structured label model literature\n67\t- Snorkel MeTaL (Ratner et al., 2019): HMM-based label models handling sequential dependencies\n68\t- WS survey literature (Zhang et al., 2022)\n69\t- Extended PRM work (OmegaPRM, outcome-conditioned reward models)\n70\t- Agent monitoring for embodied/game agents (ALFRED, BabyAI)\n71\t- LLM-as-judge beyond Zheng et al. (Prometheus, calibration-aware judge variants)\n72\t\n73\t### Moderate\n74\t\n75\t**W4. Gold set insufficient for claimed precision of results; no confidence intervals.** (R1 W3 + R2 W5 — unanimous)\n76\t\n77\tThe gold evaluation set is 400 windows with κ=0.68 overall (borderline \"substantial\"), including 18% ambiguous windows with κ=0.45. Key comparisons (Probe 0.80 vs LF Oracle 0.78 = +2pp; Probe 0.80 vs Claude CoT 0.82 = −2pp) are made without confidence intervals. At n=400, the 95% CI on macro-F1 is likely ±3–5pp, rendering these differences statistically indistinguishable from noise. Multi-seed stability (Table 9) is reported on the auto-labeled set, not the gold set. Both reviewers flag this as a significant methodological gap.\n78\t\n79\t**W5. Cross-agent transfer degradation undermines generalization claims.** (R1 W4 + R2 W4 — unanimous)\n80\t\n81\tThe SWE-agent→OpenHands transfer shows −9pp probe degradation (0.80→0.71) and −14pp LF oracle degradation. The absolute cross-agent F1 of 0.71 is below practical deployment thresholds. No adaptation or domain-shift mitigation strategy is proposed. A pooled multi-agent training experiment (train on SWE-agent + OpenHands subset) is a notable missing experiment that would substantiate the practical relevance claim.\n82\t\n83\t**W6. \"Violation of i.i.d.\" framing is partially overstated.** (R2 W3)\n84\t\n85\tFlyingSquid already models LF dependencies via triplet methods. The paper's i.i.d. critique specifically concerns temporal ordering, not LF correlation per se, but this distinction is not cleanly drawn. With 80% of LF pairs showing no meaningful temporal dynamics, the i.i.d. violation is pair-specific rather than structural. The paper should distinguish between temporal autocorrelation, leading/lagging relationships, and non-stationarity of LF reliability.\n86\t\n87\t### Minor\n88\t\n89\t**W7. Claude CoT comparison underspecified.** (R1 W5 — R2 considers less critical)\n90\tModel version, prompt template, and temperature are not specified. The 0.82 figure is near the gold ceiling given κ=0.68. Prompt sensitivity not explored.\n91\t\n92\t**W8. Architecture motivation thin.** (R1 W6)\n93\tWhy GRU outperforms Transformer on k=5 sequences is not discussed. GRU+Attn +0.6pp significance not tested.\n94\t\n95\t**W9. \"Semantic stagnation\" lacks formal definition.** (R2 M2)\n96\tThe term is introduced informally. A precise definition in terms of task-graph non-advancement or reward stagnation would strengthen the problem statement.\n97\t\n98\t**W10. Inference latency claim (4ms) should clarify scope.** (R1 M5)\n99\tThe frozen encoder (Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B, ~600M params) dominates the computational graph. Whether 4ms includes encoder forward pass, and on what hardware, should be specified.\n100\t\n101\t---\n102\t\n103\t## 4. Key Questions for Authors\n104\t\n105\t**Q1** (W1): Can you provide an ablation where temporal weighting is applied *only to LF pairs not involving outcome_bp*? This isolates whether the temporal framework provides benefit beyond outcome_bp reweighting.\n106\t\n107\t**Q2** (W2): Has a train-without-outcome_bp condition been evaluated? The current holdout experiments (Table 12) may understate the probe's training-time dependence on outcome_bp.\n108\t\n109\t**Q3** (W3): How does the temporal label model differ from Snorkel MeTaL's HMM-based structured label model and from Safranchik et al. (2020)?\n110\t\n111\t**Q4** (W4): Can you provide bootstrap 95% CIs on key gold set comparisons (Probe vs LF Oracle, Probe vs Claude CoT)?\n112\t\n113\t**Q5** (W5): Have you attempted pooled multi-agent training (SWE-agent + OpenHands subset) to evaluate whether cross-agent degradation is recoverable?\n114\t\n115\t**Q6** (W6): What specifically in the i.i.d. assumption is violated that FlyingSquid's dependency graph cannot capture — temporal ordering, non-stationarity, or autocorrelation?\n116\t\n117\t---\n118\t\n119\t## 5. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n120\t\n121\tRelative to the experiment design reference (16 planned experiments), the paper covers all 16 plus 3 additional experiments (LF temporal dynamics, human annotation protocol, early termination simulation). Coverage is comprehensive. The missing elements are not additional experiments but rather **statistical depth** (confidence intervals, significance tests) and **ablation completeness** (train-without-outcome_bp).\n122\t\n123\t---\n124\t\n125\t## 6. Scores and Consensus\n126\t\n127\t| Reviewer | Independent Score | Post-Roundtable Score | Confidence |\n128\t|----------|------------------|-----------------------|------------|\n129\t| R1 (Method + Experiments) | 3 (Weak Reject) | 3 (Weak Reject) | 4/5 |\n130\t| R2 (Novelty + Related Work) | 3 (Weak Reject) | 3 (Weak Reject) | 4/5 |\n131\t\n132\t**Consensus Score: 3 — Weak Reject**\n133\t\n134\t### Roundtable Convergence Notes\n135\t\n136\tBoth reviewers converged independently on:\n137\t- The temporal LM's empirical fragility (gain from 2 LF pairs) as the paper's most serious vulnerability\n138\t- The outcome_bp circularity concern (R1 raised; R2 adopted as co-major after cross-sharing)\n139\t- The need for confidence intervals on gold set comparisons\n140\t- Cross-agent degradation as a genuine scope limitation\n141\t\n142\tThe reviewers complement each other: R1 provides sharper experimental methodology critique (outcome_bp circularity, gold set CI, Claude CoT controls); R2 provides the novelty and positioning critique (missing sequential WS citations, i.i.d. framing). Combined, these represent both empirical and conceptual shortcomings.\n143\t\n144\tNo disagreements reached impasse. R2 considers the Claude CoT comparison issue (R1 W5) less critical than R1 does; R1 concurs with R2's related work concerns but did not independently identify them.\n145\t\n146\t---\n147\t\n148\t## 7. Overall Assessment\n149\t\n150\t**ProgressProbe addresses a genuine problem with above-average experimental discipline.** The ablation coverage is impressive, the cost-efficiency result is practically compelling, and the limitations section is commendably honest. The paper has clear applied value as a lightweight, deployable progress estimator.\n151\t\n152\t**However, the core technical novelty claim — temporal-aware label aggregation — is not adequately supported.** The gain traces to 2 LF pairs involving outcome_bp, the temporal LM is not differentiated from existing sequential weak supervision methods, and the related work is critically incomplete. The gold set evaluation lacks the statistical rigor (confidence intervals) needed to substantiate the claimed 1-2pp margins. The outcome_bp circularity raises deployment validity concerns that are not fully resolved.\n153\t\n154\t**Path to acceptance**: (a) Engage thoroughly with sequential WS literature and articulate a precise technical delta; (b) provide bootstrap CIs on all gold set comparisons; (c) include a train-without-outcome_bp ablation; (d) reframe the temporal LM contribution as a domain-specific insight rather than a general methodology; (e) either add pooled cross-agent training or narrow the scope claim. With these revisions, the paper could reach Weak Accept or Accept.\n155\t\n156\t---\n157\t\n158\t*Review conducted: 2026-05-14 | Reviewers: R1 (Method+Experiments), R2 (Novelty+Related Work) | Protocol: dream-review-full (independent → cross-share → converge → synthesize)*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Je8BnXYm3AvvVV9jmydn5n","session_id":"50f692d3-19d9-47a7-bdff-a390f713b177","uuid":"2b1b0c07-f84b-4c81-84ee-1b3cbd2d0734","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:55:23.985Z"}
