{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0171DFsAb9ZDbak3RHvgWaoj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T081656\n3\ttier: median\n4\toverall_score: weak reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T09:03:22.254212+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: Weakly-Supervised Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n9\t\n10\t**Dream ID:** 20260514T081656\n11\t**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n12\t**Date:** 2026-05-14\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## 1. Paper Summary\n17\t\n18\tThe paper introduces a weak-supervision framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories — where agents take superficially diverse but collectively unproductive actions. Three domain-specific labeling functions (command repetition, file-diff delta, outcome back-propagation) produce noisy window-level labels via majority vote with confidence scores. A lightweight GRU probe (542K params) trained on high-confidence labels (conf≥2) achieves 0.906 macro-F1 on auto-labeled data, outperforming the Repeat-3 heuristic by 6.2pp. On a 400-window human gold set (Fleiss κ=0.62), the probe reaches 0.77 F1 — above the LF oracle (0.75) and Repeat-3 (0.72), but below an LLM-as-judge baseline (0.82 F1) that costs 35× more. Cross-agent transfer from SWE-agent to OpenHands yields 0.73 F1 zero-shot, recoverable to 0.82 with 200 labeled examples. The paper positions the weak-supervision framework — not the probe — as the primary contribution.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 2. Reviewer Summaries\n23\t\n24\t### Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods & Experiments — Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence: 4/5\n25\t\n26\tR1 finds the experimental program impressively broad (16 experiments covering baselines, ablations, transfer, error analysis, interpretability, and deployment simulation). Instance-level data splitting correctly prevents cross-contamination. The \"student exceeds teacher\" result (+2pp over LF oracle on gold) and cost-accuracy Pareto analysis against LLM judges are highlighted as strengths.\n27\t\n28\tKey concerns: (1) The paper describes a GRU with learned attention (Eq. 1 + attention equations), but R1 questions whether the actual implementation matches this description, noting that all attention-based interpretability claims depend on this being accurate. (2) All results come from a single random seed with no variance reporting or significance tests; margins of 2-5pp on N=400 gold set likely fall within bootstrap confidence intervals. (3) The frozen sentence encoder producing 1024-dim embeddings is never named, and no encoder ablation exists. (4) The outcome_backpropagation LF may leak trajectory-level outcome information into window-level labels. (5) The gold set (N=400) is undersized for the narrow margins reported.\n29\t\n30\t### Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty & Related Work — Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence: 4/5\n31\t\n32\tR2 finds the problem identification practically valuable and the experimental coverage thorough. The cost-efficiency analysis (35× cheaper, 930× faster than LLM judges) gives immediate practical relevance. The error overlap analysis (187 windows probe catches that Repeat-3 misses vs. 34 reverse) is convincing evidence of complementary signal.\n33\t\n34\tKey concerns: (1) Novelty is thin — majority vote aggregation and GRU classification are both off-the-shelf; no methodological innovation in adapting data programming to sequences. (2) Related work is severely incomplete (~10 references), missing Process Reward Models (Lightman et al., Math-Shepherd), agent scaffolding (Reflexion, LATS), and advanced label models (FlyingSquid). (3) Domain specificity undermines generality claims — all LFs, evaluation, and application are SWE-bench-specific. (4) The LLM-as-Judge comparison may undersell the LLM baseline (single prompt template, no chain-of-thought). (5) \"Semantic stagnation\" potentially rebrands existing concepts without sufficient justification for new terminology.\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t## 3. Roundtable Convergence\n39\t\n40\t### 3.1 Points of Agreement (High Confidence)\n41\t\n42\tBoth reviewers converge on these findings:\n43\t\n44\t| # | Issue | Severity | Agreement |\n45\t|---|-------|----------|-----------|\n46\t| 1 | Architecture description accuracy — paper describes GRU+attention but implementation details need verification | Critical | Both R1, R2 |\n47\t| 2 | Single seed, no variance or significance tests | Critical | Both R1, R2 |\n48\t| 3 | Embedding model unnamed, no encoder ablation | Critical | Both R1, R2 |\n49\t| 4 | outcome_backpropagation LF may leak trajectory-level outcome | Major | Both R1, R2 |\n50\t| 5 | Gold set (N=400) underpowered for reported margins | Major | Both R1, R2 |\n51\t| 6 | Novelty below top-venue bar (off-the-shelf components) | Major | R2 primary, R1 concurs |\n52\t| 7 | Related work severely incomplete | Major | R2 primary |\n53\t| 8 | Domain-specific LFs limit generality claims | Moderate | R2 primary |\n54\t| 9 | No cross-agent human validation (OpenHands) | Moderate | R1 primary |\n55\t\n56\t### 3.2 Points of Agreement — Strengths\n57\t\n58\t| # | Strength | Agreement |\n59\t|---|----------|-----------|\n60\t| 1 | Exceptional experimental breadth (16 experiments, 7 baselines, 6 ablations) | Both |\n61\t| 2 | \"Student exceeds teacher\" — probe outperforms its own label source on gold | Both |\n62\t| 3 | Cost-accuracy Pareto positioning vs. LLM judges is compelling and practical | Both |\n63\t| 4 | Error analysis with FP/FN taxonomy and overlap quantification | Both |\n64\t| 5 | LF temporal dynamics (leading/synchronous/lagging indicators) | Both |\n65\t| 6 | Honest framing — architecture as \"engineering choice, not contribution\" | Both |\n66\t| 7 | Instance-level data splitting prevents cross-contamination | Both |\n67\t| 8 | Data scaling curve establishes minimum viable dataset size (~4K windows) | R1 |\n68\t\n69\t### 3.3 Resolved Disagreements\n70\t\n71\tNo score disagreements to resolve. R1 and R2 independently arrived at 3/6 with complementary but non-conflicting rationales. R1's statistical rigor concerns and R2's novelty concerns reinforce each other: thin novelty places a higher burden on experimental execution, and the execution gaps (single seed, no CIs, small gold set) fail to meet that raised bar.\n72\t\n73\t---\n74\t\n75\t## 4. Consolidated Assessment\n76\t\n77\t### 4.1 Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n78\t\n79\t### 4.2 Confidence: 4/5 (High)\n80\t\n81\t### 4.3 Justification\n82\t\n83\tThe paper tackles a genuine and practically important problem — semantic stagnation in LLM agents is a real failure mode that existing tools detect poorly. The experimental program is among the most thorough we see at this stage, covering 16 experiments with good coverage of baselines, ablations, transfer evaluation, error analysis, and practical deployment simulation. The \"student exceeds teacher\" finding and the cost-accuracy Pareto positioning against LLM judges are genuinely useful contributions.\n84\t\n85\tHowever, the paper does not meet the acceptance bar for a top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate) for the following reasons:\n86\t\n87\t1. **Novelty gap.** The methodological contribution reduces to applying two well-known techniques (Snorkel-style majority vote + GRU classifier) to a new domain. The data programming framework is used without modification — no temporal label model, no LF dependency modeling, no innovation in aggregation for sequential data. This places the paper in \"competent application\" territory, which requires exceptionally strong empirical results to compensate.\n88\t\n89\t2. **Statistical rigor below threshold.** The compensating empirical strength is undermined by: single-seed results throughout, no confidence intervals or significance tests, and a gold set (N=400) that provides ~±3.5pp bootstrap CIs — meaning the 2pp probe-vs-oracle gap and several other key margins are likely non-significant. For a paper whose value proposition is empirical, this is disqualifying.\n90\t\n91\t3. **Architecture description concerns.** The paper describes a GRU with learned attention weights and builds interpretability arguments on attention patterns (concentration at stagnation onset, entropy differences). If the actual implementation differs from this description, the interpretability claims would be unsupported. This needs clarification.\n92\t\n93\t4. **Related work gaps.** ~10 references is far below venue expectations. The omission of Process Reward Models, agent scaffolding systems (Reflexion, LATS), and advanced label models (FlyingSquid) leaves the paper poorly positioned. The relationship to PRMs — which also estimate step-level quality in sequential decision-making — is never discussed.\n94\t\n95\t5. **Information leakage concern.** The outcome_backpropagation LF uses trajectory-level outcome to label individual windows, potentially allowing the probe to learn trajectory-position and exit-status proxies rather than genuine window-level progress signals. The paper does not quantify or control for this.\n96\t\n97\t### 4.4 Relationship to Experimental Design\n98\t\n99\tThe paper covers all experiments from the experimental design: full-data training, LF oracle, LLM-as-judge, MeanPool+LR, error analysis, cross-agent transfer, window size ablation, architecture ablation, LF hold-one-out, SupCon ablation, non-NLP baseline, data scaling, early termination simulation, interpretability analysis, human gold set, and LF temporal dynamics. Coverage is complete and well-organized across main text and appendix.\n100\t\n101\tThe experimental results are internally consistent with the design expectations. Key numbers closely match planned values (e.g., conf≥2 F1=0.906, full-data F1=0.82, zero-shot transfer F1=0.73, gold F1=0.77). The narrative adjustment from \"probe is strong\" to \"framework enables research, probe is one efficient downstream application\" is executed as designed.\n102\t\n103\t---\n104\t\n105\t## 5. Actionable Recommendations for Revision\n106\t\n107\t### Must-fix (for any resubmission):\n108\t\n109\t1. **Multi-seed results.** Run all main experiments with ≥3 seeds. Report mean ± std. Provide bootstrap CIs on gold-set comparisons.\n110\t\n111\t2. **Name the embedding model.** State the frozen encoder explicitly. Run at least one alternative encoder (e.g., BGE, E5, GTE) to assess sensitivity.\n112\t\n113\t3. **Clarify architecture.** Ensure the paper description exactly matches the implementation. If attention is used, the code should reflect it. If not, remove attention-based interpretability claims and rerun architecture ablations.\n114\t\n115\t4. **Expand related work.** Add PRMs (Lightman et al., Math-Shepherd), agent scaffolding (Reflexion, LATS, Tree of Thoughts), advanced label models (FlyingSquid), and execution feedback systems. Discuss relationship to PRMs explicitly. Target 30+ references.\n116\t\n117\t5. **Quantify outcome_backpropagation impact.** Report what fraction of training data comes from exit_context trajectories. Show results with outcome_backpropagation restricted to last-N-windows only (no blanket labeling).\n118\t\n119\t### Should-fix:\n120\t\n121\t6. **Larger gold set.** Scale to ≥1000 windows to tighten CIs on the 2-5pp margins that drive key claims.\n122\t\n123\t7. **Stronger LLM baseline.** Test chain-of-thought prompting for LLM-as-judge. The current single-template comparison may undersell the LLM.\n124\t\n125\t8. **Soft-match Repeat-3.** Implement edit-distance or embedding-similarity variant of Repeat-3 to make the comparison fairer.\n126\t\n127\t9. **Cross-domain signal.** Even a small-scale evaluation on non-SWE-bench trajectories (web navigation, research tasks) would substantially strengthen the generality claim.\n128\t\n129\t10. **Discuss PRM relationship.** Frame ProgressProbe relative to PRMs — what does weak supervision on trajectory structure buy over training a PRM with outcome supervision?\n130\t\n131\t### Nice-to-have:\n132\t\n133\t11. Consider using Snorkel's generative label model or FlyingSquid instead of naive majority vote, given low inter-LF agreement (LF agreement at onset = 0.35).\n134\t\n135\t12. Explore temporal confidence weighting (weight by LF lead/lag position rather than static confidence scores).\n136\t\n137\t---\n138\t\n139\t## 6. Meta-Assessment\n140\t\n141\tThis paper is a solid empirical study of a real problem, held back by insufficient novelty for a top venue and execution gaps in statistical rigor. The experimental design is comprehensive and the framing is honest. With the multi-seed results, named encoder, corrected architecture description, and expanded related work, it could become a competitive submission — likely as an empirical/applications paper rather than a methods paper. The weak-supervision framework for trajectory annotation is a genuine practical contribution even if it doesn't clear a high novelty bar; the right venue and framing could make this publishable.\n142\t\n143\t**Recommended venue positioning:** Workshop paper at a top venue (NeurIPS/ICML workshop on agents or data-centric AI), or full paper at a mid-tier venue (AAMAS, EACL). For top-venue acceptance, a methodological contribution to the label aggregation step (temporal label model) would be needed.\n144\t\n145\t---\n146\t\n147\t*Review conducted by: R1 (Methods & Experiments) + R2 (Novelty & Related Work)*\n148\t*Roundtable: 4-step protocol (Independent → Cross-share → Convergence → Synthesis)*\n149\t*Consensus score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence 4/5*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Je8BnXYm3AvvVV9jmydn5n","session_id":"50f692d3-19d9-47a7-bdff-a390f713b177","uuid":"bae211b7-37aa-4e24-8940-84f128e2ac80","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:55:24.336Z"}
