{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A45Qiyy6rPzdXBrg8KJrcF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T095527\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: weak accept\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T10:46:31.597085+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: Temporal Label Models for Estimating Progress in LLM Agent Trajectories\n9\t\n10\t**Dream ID:** 20260514T095527\n11\t**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n12\t**Date:** 2026-05-14\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Roundtable Summary\n17\t\n18\tTwo reviewers independently assessed this paper — R1 focusing on Methods and Experiments, R2 on Novelty and Related Work. Both converged on **Weak Accept (4/6)**, agreeing that the temporal label model is a genuine methodological step up from prior work with thorough experimental support, but that novelty is incremental, a circularity concern in the core temporal dynamics analysis is inadequately addressed, and significant related work is missing.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n23\t\n24\t### Summary\n25\tThe paper proposes a Temporal Label Model (TLM), an HMM that models leading/concurrent/lagging temporal roles of labeling functions on sequential agent trajectory data, and a lightweight GRU probe (ProgressProbe) trained on TLM labels for detecting semantic stagnation. On a 412-window human gold set (κ=0.82), TLM improves label quality by 12.2pp over majority vote and 5.9pp over FlyingSquid, while ProgressProbe achieves 0.858 macro-F1, outperforming the LF oracle by 6.0pp and LLM-as-judge (CoT) by 4.5pp at 690× lower latency.\n26\t\n27\t### Strengths\n28\t\n29\t1. **Well-motivated temporal label model with clean ablation ladder.** The MV → FlyingSquid → TLM progression provides a clear ablation of what temporal modeling adds beyond correlation-aware aggregation. The +5.9pp over FlyingSquid specifically isolates the temporal modeling contribution.\n30\t\n31\t2. **Unusually comprehensive experiments.** The paper covers ~20 anticipated reviewer concerns: multi-seed stability, outcome leakage sensitivity, data scaling, encoder ablation, LF hold-one-out, architecture ablation, window size sweep, non-NLP structural baseline, CosineSim threshold analysis, full-data (conf≥1) training, error analysis, interpretability, and early termination simulation. This is well above average for a submission.\n32\t\n33\t3. **Compelling cross-agent transfer asymmetry.** The probe degrading by only −6.7pp vs. the LF oracle collapsing by −21.6pp when transferring from SWE-agent to OpenHands is the paper's most convincing result. The per-LF breakdown (command_repetition to 0.491, edit_churn to 0.385) provides mechanistic explanation.\n34\t\n35\t4. **Honest outcome leakage treatment.** The paper directly addresses the potential information leakage from outcome_backpropagation with hold-out analysis (−1.2pp) and last-N-only variants (−0.4pp), demonstrating the signal is largely redundant with leading/concurrent LFs.\n36\t\n37\t5. **Strong gold set design.** 412 windows, 3 annotators, Fleiss' κ=0.823 with 91% majority agreement. Disagreements localized to transition boundaries (±1 step from onset/offset), which is expected and informative.\n38\t\n39\t### Weaknesses\n40\t\n41\t1. **Circularity in Table 1 temporal dynamics analysis.** Stagnation onset is defined by the same LFs whose temporal roles are being measured. If command_repetition dominates the majority-vote onset definition, its \"leading\" role (−1.82 steps) is partly tautological — it fires first because it partly defines when onset occurs. The paper should validate temporal dynamics against human-annotated onset windows, even on a subset.\n42\t\n43\t2. **Gold set overuse without confidence intervals.** The 412-window gold set simultaneously serves as: evaluation benchmark, TLM validation target, ablation basis, error analysis sample, and interpretability visualization source. No bootstrap confidence intervals are reported for any comparison on this set. With N=412, the 95% CI for a macro-F1 of 0.858 is roughly ±0.03, which overlaps with several baselines. McNemar's test or paired bootstrap should be used for the central claims (TLM vs. FlyingSquid, probe vs. LF oracle, probe vs. LLM-as-judge).\n44\t\n45\t3. **Unclear OpenHands ground truth.** Table 5 reports F1 on OpenHands trajectories, but the paper never specifies what ground truth these predictions are evaluated against. Since the LFs are shown to degrade catastrophically on OpenHands (command_repetition F1=0.491), LF-derived labels cannot serve as reliable ground truth. Is there a separate human annotation set for OpenHands? If evaluation uses degraded LF labels, the reported F1 values are unreliable.\n46\t\n47\t4. **Missing significance tests on central comparisons.** The paper reports no statistical significance tests for its three headline claims: TLM vs. FlyingSquid (+5.9pp), probe vs. LF oracle (+6.0pp), probe vs. LLM-as-judge (+4.5pp). On a 412-window gold set, these gaps may or may not be statistically significant. Paired bootstrap or McNemar's test is standard practice.\n48\t\n49\t5. **TLM mathematical formulation is informal.** For a methods contribution, Section 3.4 describes the HMM in bullet points without formal equations for the transition matrix, emission model, or inference procedure. The emission model description — \"Each LF's vote is conditioned on the hidden state and its learned temporal role. Leading LFs receive upweighted influence near state transitions\" — leaves the parameterization unclear.\n50\t\n51\t### Questions for Authors\n52\t\n53\t1. Can you report temporal dynamics (Table 1 offsets) using human-annotated onset windows from the gold set, even on the available subset?\n54\t2. What is the ground truth for the OpenHands cross-agent evaluation in Table 5?\n55\t3. Can you provide bootstrap 95% CIs for the main comparisons on the gold set?\n56\t4. The \"static baseline\" in the ΔBIC=−342.7 comparison — is this a time-invariant HMM, FlyingSquid, or MV? The interpretation differs substantially.\n57\t5. What is the class distribution in conf=1 vs. conf≥2 regions? If conf=1 is heavily skewed, TLM's improvement could partly reflect better prior estimation rather than temporal modeling.\n58\t6. Table 6 shows GRU+mean-pool (0.902) and Transformer (0.908) only on conf≥2, with no conf≥1 or gold set numbers. Were these evaluated on the gold set? If so, what are the numbers?\n59\t\n60\t### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n61\t\n62\t- Bootstrap CIs or McNemar's test on all gold set comparisons\n63\t- Temporal dynamics validated on human-annotated onsets\n64\t- OpenHands ground truth specification and potentially a small human-annotated OpenHands gold set\n65\t- Per-class (progress/stagnation) F1 breakdown for TLM vs. MV vs. FlyingSquid on gold set\n66\t- Learning curves for the TLM itself (how many trajectories needed for EM convergence?)\n67\t\n68\t### Minor Issues\n69\t\n70\t- Table 7 reports parameter counts for GRU+attention (847K) and Transformer (2.13M), but not for GRU+mean-pool or MLP — inconsistent.\n71\t- Section A.2 and A.3 headers (\"Encoder Ablation\", \"LF Hold-One-Out\") appear but have no text — only tables. These should have at least a brief description.\n72\t- The paper uses \"macro-F1\" and \"F1\" interchangeably in some places. Given class imbalance (248:164), standardize to \"macro-F1.\"\n73\t\n74\t### Confidence Score\n75\t4\n76\t\n77\t### Preliminary Score\n78\tScore: 4 — Thorough experimental work with a well-motivated temporal label model, but circularity in the core dynamics analysis, missing significance tests, unclear OpenHands ground truth, and informal method formalization prevent a confident accept.\n79\t\n80\t---\n81\t\n82\t## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n83\t\n84\t### Summary\n85\tThe paper proposes a Temporal Label Model (TLM), an HMM-based extension of data programming that models leading/concurrent/lagging temporal roles of labeling functions on sequential agent trajectory data, combined with a lightweight GRU probe (ProgressProbe) for detecting semantic stagnation. The core novelty claim is that standard weak supervision violates i.i.d. assumptions on sequential data and that explicitly modeling LF temporal dynamics yields substantially better label aggregation (+12.2pp over majority vote, +5.9pp over FlyingSquid). The \"student exceeds teacher\" finding (+6.0pp over LF oracle) and cross-agent transferability (+14.9pp advantage over LF oracle in degradation gap) are positioned as empirical validation of the learned representations.\n86\t\n87\t### Strengths\n88\t\n89\t1. **The temporal label model is a genuine methodological contribution.** Unlike vanilla majority vote, TLM addresses a real gap: standard data programming assumes i.i.d. data points, but LFs on sequential agent data fire at systematically different temporal offsets. Modeling this via an HMM with emission roles tied to LF temporal dynamics is a principled and non-obvious extension. The +5.9pp improvement over FlyingSquid — which already models LF correlations but not temporal structure — provides clean evidence that temporal modeling adds value beyond correlation-aware aggregation.\n90\t\n91\t2. **The empirical characterization of LF temporal dynamics is rigorous and actionable.** The analysis over 3,847 stagnation onset windows with statistical testing (p<0.001 for leading/lagging roles, Granger causality confirmation) elevates this from descriptive observation to prescriptive methodology.\n92\t\n93\t3. **\"Student exceeds teacher\" is well-supported and meaningful.** The +6.0pp gap over the LF oracle on a human gold set (κ=0.82) is practically significant. The explanation — that the probe leverages embedding-space patterns in the conf=1 region where individual LFs are unreliable — is mechanistically plausible.\n94\t\n95\t4. **Cross-agent transfer provides strong generalization evidence.** The asymmetry between probe degradation (−6.7pp) and LF oracle degradation (−21.6pp) is the paper's most convincing result. The per-LF breakdown provides mechanistic explanation.\n96\t\n97\t5. **Comprehensive experimental design with appropriate controls.** FlyingSquid baseline, LLM-as-judge with CoT variants, encoder ablation, multi-seed stability, outcome leakage analysis — the experimental coverage is thorough and well-organized.\n98\t\n99\t6. **The cost-accuracy Pareto argument is compelling.** 690× faster and ~690× cheaper than LLM-as-judge while achieving +4.5pp higher F1 is a clear practical contribution.\n100\t\n101\t### Weaknesses\n102\t\n103\t1. **TLM is a relatively straightforward HMM application, and the novelty may be narrower than claimed.** The method is a 2-state HMM with EM training — a textbook model. The \"temporal label model\" framing suggests a general framework, but the paper demonstrates it only on 4 hand-crafted LFs in one domain with a binary classification task. The paper should formalize what makes TLM different from simply running an HMM on LF outputs.\n104\t\n105\t2. **Circularity concern in LF temporal dynamics measurement remains partially unresolved.** Stagnation onset is defined by the same LFs whose temporal roles are being measured. The paper should explicitly address this circularity, ideally by reporting temporal dynamics relative to human-annotated onset windows.\n106\t\n107\t3. **The generalization claim for TLM \"beyond agent monitoring\" is unsupported.** Section 6 claims TLM applies to \"any weak supervision task on time series, event logs, or dialogue.\" This is speculative — no evidence is provided. Either demonstrate on a second task or substantially tone down the claim.\n108\t\n109\t4. **Two agents in one domain is narrow for the transferability claim.** SWE-agent and OpenHands both operate on SWE-bench software engineering tasks with similar action spaces. This tests format robustness, not domain generalization. The claim should be scoped to \"cross-implementation transfer within a single task domain.\"\n110\t\n111\t5. **The relationship to Process Reward Models needs deeper treatment.** PRMs can also be trained with outcome-based supervision without per-step labels (Math-Shepherd). The paper should address whether an outcome-supervised PRM could match ProgressProbe, and what the window-level formulation buys over aggregated step-level PRM scores.\n112\t\n113\t6. **HMM-based label models have been explored in adjacent areas but are not cited.** The crowdsourcing literature has explored HMM-based sequential label aggregation models (Nguyen et al. 2017, Simpson & Gurevych 2019, Rodrigues et al. 2018). These model annotator temporal dependencies in sequence labeling and are direct precursors.\n114\t\n115\t### Questions for Authors\n116\t\n117\t1. How does the temporal dynamics analysis change when stagnation onset is defined by human annotations rather than LF majority vote?\n118\t2. What is the \"static baseline\" in the BIC comparison (ΔBIC = −342.7)?\n119\t3. How exactly is the temporal role parameterized in the emission model?\n120\t4. What is the class distribution in conf=1 vs. conf≥2 regions?\n121\t5. In the cross-agent experiment, is TLM retrained on OpenHands LF votes, or is the evaluation purely zero-shot on the probe side?\n122\t6. Could you report per-class F1 for TLM vs. MV labels on the gold set?\n123\t7. Given outcome_bp's minimal contribution (−1.2pp when held out) and potential leakage, why include it at all?\n124\t\n125\t### Missing Related Work\n126\t\n127\t1. **Sequential crowdsourcing and annotator models:** Nguyen et al. (2017), Simpson & Gurevych (2019), Rodrigues et al. (2018) — model annotator temporal dependencies in sequence labeling, directly analogous to modeling LF temporal roles.\n128\t2. **Temporal extensions of data programming:** Varma & Ré (2018) Snuba; any work on applying Snorkel to sequential/temporal data. The claim of being the first temporal extension needs verification.\n129\t3. **Automatic PRM training:** Math-Shepherd, GenPRM, OmegaPRM — reduces annotation cost similarly.\n130\t4. **Agent trajectory evaluation benchmarks:** AgentBench, MINT, SWE-bench Verified.\n131\t5. **Stagnation/deadlock detection in classical agent systems:** Livelock detection in planning/robotics.\n132\t6. **Noisy label learning:** DivideMix, co-teaching, symmetric cross-entropy — alternatives at the model training stage.\n133\t7. **Reward redistribution:** RUDDER, HCA — temporal credit assignment in sequential decision-making.\n134\t\n135\t### Minor Issues\n136\t\n137\t- The abstract states \"690× faster\" and the LLM-as-judge table uses a single cost ($4.82/1k) for all LLM configurations. GPT-4o and Claude Sonnet have different pricing — note this approximation.\n138\t- Table 2 mixes label model accuracy and model prediction F1 in the same column without clear delineation.\n139\t- Section 3.4 (the core method) lacks formal equations for a methods paper.\n140\t- References are thin at 16 entries (vs. 25–35 typical for this intersection). The missing related work above would naturally expand this.\n141\t- Some bibliography entries are incomplete: Li et al. (2024) TrajAD and Zhang et al. (2024) SpecRA lack venue information. Citation existence should be verified.\n142\t\n143\t### Confidence Score\n144\t4\n145\t\n146\t### Preliminary Score\n147\tScore: 4 — The temporal label model is a meaningful methodological step up from vanilla majority vote, the empirical results are thorough with appropriate controls, and the cross-agent transfer result is genuinely informative. However, the TLM is a relatively standard HMM application, the generalization claims outrun the evidence (two agents, one domain), circularity in temporal dynamics measurement is inadequately addressed, and key related work (sequential crowdsourcing models, automatic PRM training, noisy label learning) is missing.\n148\t\n149\t---\n150\t\n151\t## Roundtable: Cross-Sharing and Convergence\n152\t\n153\t### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n154\t\n155\t**R1 → R2:** R1 raised concerns about (a) missing significance tests on central comparisons, (b) gold set overuse without CIs, (c) unclear OpenHands ground truth, and (d) informal TLM mathematical formulation. R1 agreed with R2 on the circularity concern.\n156\t\n157\t**R2 → R1:** R2 raised concerns about (a) TLM novelty being a straightforward HMM, (b) unsupported generalization claims beyond agent monitoring, (c) narrow transferability scope, (d) superficial PRM comparison, and (e) missing related work in sequential crowdsourcing. R2 agreed with R1 on experimental thoroughness as a strength.\n158\t\n159\t### Step 3: Convergence\n160\t\n161\t**Points of agreement:**\n162\t- Both reviewers assign **4/6 (Weak Accept)**\n163\t- **Circularity in Table 1** is the most critical methodological concern — onset defined by same LFs whose roles are measured\n164\t- **Cross-agent transfer** is the paper's strongest and most convincing result\n165\t- **Experimental thoroughness** is commendable and above average\n166\t- **Gold set quality** (κ=0.82) is strong, though both note limitations in its use\n167\t- **TLM is a real contribution** over majority vote, but the magnitude of novelty is debatable\n168\t\n169\t**Points of disagreement/unique emphasis:**\n170\t- R1 emphasizes statistical rigor gaps (no CIs, no significance tests) as potentially affecting whether the central claims hold; R2 considers the effect sizes large enough to be robust but concedes formal testing is needed\n171\t- R2 is more concerned about novelty (standard HMM) and missing related work; R1 is more concerned about methodological execution and reproducibility\n172\t- R2 uniquely flags the unsupported \"TLM generalizes broadly\" claim and the superficial PRM comparison\n173\t- R1 uniquely flags the unclear OpenHands evaluation ground truth\n174\t\n175\t**Resolution:** Both reviewers maintain 4/6 after cross-sharing. R1 notes that if significance tests were provided and the circularity addressed, the score could move to 5. R2 notes that if the missing related work (especially sequential crowdsourcing HMMs) reveals TLM to be previously proposed, the score would drop to 3.\n176\t\n177\t---\n178\t\n179\t## Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n180\t\n181\t### Overall Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)\n182\t\n183\t### Decision Rationale\n184\t\n185\tThis paper makes a genuine methodological contribution by extending data programming to sequential settings via a temporal label model. The key insight — that labeling functions on agent trajectories exhibit systematic temporal roles (leading/lagging) that violate the i.i.d. assumption — is well-supported empirically and leads to a principled HMM-based solution with clear improvements over both majority vote (+12.2pp) and the correlation-aware FlyingSquid (+5.9pp). The experimental coverage is unusually thorough, covering ~20 ablations and analyses that pre-emptively address most anticipated concerns.\n186\t\n187\tHowever, several issues prevent a confident accept:\n188\t\n189\t### Critical Issues (must-fix for acceptance)\n190\t\n191\t1. **Circularity in temporal dynamics analysis (both reviewers).** Validate LF temporal roles against human-annotated onset windows, not LF-defined onsets. Even a subset analysis on the gold set would suffice.\n192\t\n193\t2. **Missing significance tests (R1, endorsed by R2).** Provide paired bootstrap CIs or McNemar's test for the three central claims: TLM vs. FlyingSquid, probe vs. LF oracle, probe vs. LLM-as-judge.\n194\t\n195\t3. **OpenHands ground truth specification (R1).** Clarify what ground truth is used for Table 5. If LF-derived labels, acknowledge the limitation explicitly given documented LF degradation.\n196\t\n197\t4. **Formalize TLM (both reviewers).** Add equations for the transition matrix, emission model parameterization, and inference procedure. The current bullet-point description is insufficient for a methods contribution.\n198\t\n199\t### Important Issues (strongly encouraged)\n200\t\n201\t5. **Cite and differentiate from sequential crowdsourcing models (R2).** HMM-based annotator models in crowdsourcing (Nguyen et al. 2017, Simpson & Gurevych 2019) are direct precursors and must be discussed.\n202\t\n203\t6. **Scope generalization claims (R2).** Either provide evidence for TLM on a second sequential task or reframe the Discussion claim as a hypothesis.\n204\t\n205\t7. **Deepen PRM comparison (R2).** Address whether outcome-supervised PRMs (Math-Shepherd) could serve as an alternative approach.\n206\t\n207\t8. **Report per-class F1 and class distribution by confidence level (both reviewers).**\n208\t\n209\t### What Would Change the Score\n210\t\n211\t- **To 5 (Accept):** Address items 1–4, cite sequential crowdsourcing literature, add a brief second-domain demonstration of TLM.\n212\t- **To 3 (Weak Reject):** If sequential crowdsourcing HMMs are found to subsume TLM, or if significance tests reveal that TLM vs. FlyingSquid or probe vs. LF oracle gaps are not statistically significant.\n213\t\n214\t### Experiment Coverage Assessment\n215\t\n216\tThe paper covers all 18+ imagined experiments from the design context, which is exceptional. Every major analysis (temporal dynamics, label model ladder, cross-agent transfer, LF hold-one-out, outcome leakage, architecture/encoder/window ablations, error analysis, interpretability, early termination, data scaling, non-NLP baseline, multi-seed stability) is present in either the main text or appendix. Coverage is **complete**.\n217\t\n218\t### Strengths Summary\n219\t1. Temporal label model is a principled extension of data programming to sequential settings\n220\t2. Clean MV → FlyingSquid → TLM ablation ladder isolating temporal modeling contribution\n221\t3. Cross-agent transfer asymmetry (−6.7pp probe vs. −21.6pp LF oracle) is highly convincing\n222\t4. Exceptional experimental breadth (~20 analyses)\n223\t5. Strong gold set (κ=0.82, 412 windows, 3 annotators)\n224\t6. Compelling cost-accuracy Pareto vs. LLM-as-judge (690× faster, +4.5pp F1)\n225\t\n226\t### Weaknesses Summary\n227\t1. Circularity in temporal dynamics measurement (onset defined by same LFs)\n228\t2. No significance tests or CIs on central comparisons\n229\t3. TLM novelty is incremental (standard 2-state HMM with EM)\n230\t4. Informal method formalization (no equations for core contribution)\n231\t5. Missing key related work (sequential crowdsourcing HMMs, automatic PRMs)\n232\t6. Narrow transferability scope (2 SE agents) presented as broad generalization\n233\t7. Unclear OpenHands evaluation ground truth\n234\t8. Unsupported claim that TLM generalizes to arbitrary sequential weak supervision tasks\n235\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Je8BnXYm3AvvVV9jmydn5n","session_id":"50f692d3-19d9-47a7-bdff-a390f713b177","uuid":"3c9c9da1-f6be-4e78-a5c2-9b1268779ed9","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:55:31.458Z"}
